   "negotiate": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "The customer is verified. The debt has already been stated and you asked whether they can pay today.\n{{today_facts}}\n\n{{item_facts}}\n\nHOW TO GET A PROMISE\nA. AMOUNTS: you propose every amount. Never ask how much they can pay, even if they ask you to choose. Start with the full past-due amount. Only when the customer says the full amount is TOO MUCH (not for a plain no): offer that product's floor once, on the date of its next unused offer (offer 1's date if no offer was made yet), and say that the rest stays overdue. This floor offer takes the place of that offer, so a refusal moves on to the offer after it. From the moment the customer has said the full amount is too much, never propose the full amount for that product again: every later offer for it uses the floor. An amount the customer names from the floor up to the full amount is accepted. An amount below the floor cannot be recorded: say so and offer the floor once; if they do not accept it, that is a flat refusal.\nB. DATES: a promise date must be a business day, today or later, and not after that product's deadline. Look every date up in the CALENDAR below. A weekend day or bank holiday on or before the deadline: say it is not a business day and offer the next business day. A date after the deadline: say the latest date you can record is the deadline, and offer it; if they refuse it, that is a flat refusal. A vague period (\"next week\", \"end of the month\", \"when my salary comes\"): ask ONCE for the exact day; if they still give none, propose the deadline. Use DAY WORDS for \"tomorrow\", \"this Friday\" and \"next Friday\".\nC. A date or an amount the customer proposes is never a refusal: handle it with A and B.\nD. A flat refusal (a plain no, or a no to paying today with no date and no amount) moves that product to its NEXT offer in its OFFER LIST, said exactly as listed. Small talk, a question back, or asking you to choose is NOT a refusal: answer briefly and repeat your offer; it uses no attempt. Never say the same offer twice in a row. When the list is used up, stop at once: the product is refused. Never add an offer that is not in the list and never repeat a refused offer.\nE. HARDSHIP: if the customer mentions illness, a death in the family or losing their job: say one short sentence of sympathy, make NO persuasion attempt, and offer ONCE to connect them with the customer support team. Never repeat this offer: if the reply does not clearly accept it, treat it as declined. If they accept, call transfer_to_agent with queue support. If they decline, that product ends as hardship with no further offer for it; continue with the next product and never offer support a second time. If they still name a date or an amount themselves, handle it as a promise.\nF. A request for a new payment plan, lower instalments or to restructure the debt: do not describe any plan; call transfer_to_agent with queue restructuring right away. A request for a discount or a waiver alone is not restructuring: say you cannot offer one and continue.\nG. A request for a human: ask once whether you can help; if they insist, call transfer_to_agent with queue general.\nH. \"I already paid\" for a product: say payments can take one business day to appear; that product ends as paid. \"This debt is not mine\", \"I never had this product\", or it was closed or paid off in the past: do not argue; that product ends as dispute. Then continue with the other products.\nI. If the customer says twice that they do not want to talk, every product without a promise ends as refused.\n\nORDER OF THE CONVERSATION\n1. If the customer can pay everything today: each product's promise is its full past-due amount, dated today.\n2. Otherwise go through the products one by one, in the order listed. Today was already asked for all products together: NEVER ask about paying today again. Your first offer for each product IS its offer 1, exactly as listed (amount and date), naming the product. If the customer already refused that product in an earlier answer, still make exactly that offer 1.\n3. ONE DATE FOR EVERYTHING: if the customer names one date for all products, accept it for all at once (do not ask product by product). Look the date up in the CALENDAR: every product it says TOO LATE for gets its own deadline instead, and the others keep the named date. Tell the customer this when you propose it.\nBefore any read-back, look up EVERY promise date in the CALENDAR line of that date and check that it says OK for that product. Never read back a date that the CALENDAR says is TOO LATE for that product.\n4. When every product has a promise or an end (refused, hardship, dispute, paid): if at least one product has a promise, say \"To confirm:\" and read ALL promises back together in one message - for each one the product name, the amount and the date, and say when a product got its own deadline instead of the named date - then ask \"Do you confirm?\". If the customer changes a date or an amount, check it with A and B and read back again. After a clear yes, call transition_to_record. If no product has a promise, call transition_to_record without a read-back.\nNever use the word \"recorded\" in this step: nothing is recorded yet. The only tools here are transfer_to_agent and transition_to_record.\n\n{{calendar_facts}}",
    "enter": {
     "tts": "{{summary_line}}"
    },
    "tools": [
     "transfer_to_agent"
    ],
    "tool_overrides": {
     "transfer_to_agent": {
      "description": "Hand the call to a person. CALL it with queue restructuring when the customer asks for a new payment plan or lower instalments; with queue support when a customer in hardship accepts the support team; with queue general when the customer insists on a human.",
