   },
   "compute_facts": {
    "type": "compute",
    "next": "negotiate",
    "fail": "end:account_error"
   },
   "negotiate": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "The customer is verified. The late payments have already been stated, one sentence per product, and you asked whether they can pay all of it today. Never use the word debt; say outstanding balance.\n{{today_facts}}\n\n{{item_facts}}\n\nHOW TO GET A PROMISE\nA. AMOUNTS: you propose every amount. Never ask how much they can pay, even if they ask you to choose. Start with the full past-due amount. Only when the customer says the full amount is TOO MUCH (not for a plain no): offer that product's floor once, on the date of its next unused attempt (attempt 1's date if no attempt was made yet), and say that the rest stays overdue. This floor offer takes the place of that attempt, so a refusal moves on to the attempt after it. From the moment the customer has said the full amount is too much, never propose the full amount for that product again. An amount the customer names from the floor up to the full amount is accepted. An amount below the floor cannot be recorded: say so and offer the floor once; if they do not accept it, that is a flat refusal.\nB. DATES: a promise date must be a business day, today or later, and not after that product's deadline. Look every date up in the CALENDAR below. A weekend day or bank holiday on or before the deadline: say it is not a business day and offer the next business day. A date after the deadline: say the latest date is the deadline, and offer it; if they refuse it, that is a flat refusal. A vague period (for example next week, end of the month, when my salary comes): ask ONCE for the exact day; if they still give none, propose the deadline. Next <weekday> means that weekday of the following calendar week; this <weekday> means the current week. Use the DAY WORDS in the CALENDAR.\nC. A date or an amount the customer proposes is never a refusal: handle it with A and B.\nD. A flat refusal or a non-answer moves that product to its NEXT attempt in its OFFER LIST, said exactly as listed. Small talk, a question back, or asking you to choose is NOT a refusal: answer briefly and repeat the same offer; it uses no attempt. Never say the same offer twice in a row after a refusal. When the list is used up, stop at once: the product is refused. Never add an offer that is not in the list. No discounts, waivers or plans.\nE. HARDSHIP: if the customer mentions illness, bereavement or job loss: say one short sentence of sympathy, make NO persuasion attempt, and offer ONCE to connect them with the support team. Never repeat this offer: if the reply does not clearly accept it, treat it as declined. If they accept, call transfer_to_agent with queue support. If they decline, that product ends as hardship with no further offer for it; continue with the next product and never offer the support team a second time. If they still name a date or an amount themselves, handle it as a promise.\nF. A request for a new plan or lower instalments: do not describe any plan; call transfer_to_agent with queue restructuring at once. A request for a discount or a waiver alone is not that: say you cannot offer one and continue.\nG. A request for a human: ask once whether you can help; if they insist, call transfer_to_agent with queue general.\nH. Already paid for a product: say payments can take one business day to appear; that product ends as paid. Not mine, never had it, or closed or paid off in the past: do not argue; that product ends as dispute. Then continue with the other products.\nI. If the customer says twice that they do not want to talk, every product without a promise ends as refused: call transition_to_record.\n\nORDER OF THE CONVERSATION\n1. If the customer can pay everything today: each product's promise is its full past-due amount, dated today (if today is a business day; otherwise the next business day).\n2. Otherwise go through the products one by one, in the order listed. Today was already asked for all products together: NEVER ask about paying today again. Your first offer for each product after a refusal IS its attempt 1, exactly as listed (amount and date), naming the product. If the customer already refused that product in an earlier answer, still make exactly that attempt 1.\n3. ONE DATE FOR EVERYTHING: if the customer names one date for all products, accept it for all at once. Look the date up in the CALENDAR: every product it says TOO LATE for gets its own deadline instead, and the others keep the named date. Tell the customer this when you propose it.\nBefore any read-back, look up EVERY promise date in the CALENDAR and check that it says OK for that product. Never read back a date that the CALENDAR says is TOO LATE for that product.\n4. When every product has a promise or an end (refused, hardship, dispute, paid): if at least one product has a promise, say \"To confirm:\" and read ALL promises back together in ONE message - for each the product name, the amount and the date, and say when a product got its own deadline instead of the named date - then ask \"Do you confirm?\". If the customer changes a date or an amount, check it with A and B and read back again. After a clear yes, call transition_to_record. If no product has a promise, call transition_to_record without a read-back.\nNever use the word recorded in this step: nothing is recorded yet. The only tools here are transfer_to_agent and transition_to_record.\n\n{{calendar_facts}}",
    "enter": {"tts": "{{summary_line}}"},
    "tools": ["transfer_to_agent"],
    "tool_overrides": {
     "transfer_to_agent": {
      "description": "Hand the call to a person. CALL it with queue restructuring when the customer asks for a new plan or lower instalments; with queue support when a customer in hardship accepts the support team; with queue general when the customer insists on a human.",
      "fields": {"queue": {"enum": ["restructuring", "support", "general"]}}
     }
    },
    "turns": 45,
    "overflow": "record",
    "routes": [
     {"to": "end:transfer", "when": {"set": "transfer_queue"}, "description": "The call was transferred"},
     {"to": "record", "tool_call": true, "description": "The customer clearly confirmed the read-back of the promises (or no product has a promise and every product has its end)."}
    ],
    "offer_state": {
     "floor_field": "floor",
     "full_field": "due",
     "hardship_triggers": "illness, bereavement, job loss",
     "hardship_offer": "offer the support team"
    }
   },
   "record": {
    "type": "converse",
    "task": "Record what was agreed. Products:\n{{item_facts}}\n- For each product whose promise the customer confirmed in the read-back: call create_promise once, with its product_id, the amount and the date (YYYY-MM-DD) exactly as confirmed.\n- For each product without a promise: call add_note with its product_id and its reason: refused, hardship, dispute, paid, or service_error (when create_promise failed twice for it). In text, one short sentence why.\n- The customer has already confirmed: never ask for confirmation again in this step. If create_promise fails once, call it again at once with the same values, without asking the customer.\n- One tool call per message. After each tool result, obey what_to_do_next.\n- Never say a promise is recorded unless the tool result says it IS recorded. If a promise could not be recorded, tell the customer plainly.\n- Never use the word debt.\n- When what_to_do_next says every product is handled: tell the customer in one or two sentences what was recorded and what could not be, then call transition_to_end_done.",
    "enter": {
     "llm": "The customer already confirmed the read-back. Your next message must be ONLY a tool call, with no words at all: call create_promise now for the first product with a confirmed promise, in product order, with exactly the amount and the date of the confirmed read-back. If no product has a promise, call add_note for the first product instead. Never ask for confirmation again, and never say recorded before a tool result says it IS recorded."
    },
    "tools": ["create_promise", "add_note"],
    "turns": 15,
    "overflow": "end:done",
    "routes": [
     {"to": "end:done", "tool_call": true, "description": "Every product is handled: what_to_do_next said so and the customer was told what was recorded.", "done": true}
    ]
   }
  }
 },
